<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        Semantic colours AND the brushes views bind to, one complete set per
        theme.

        ADR-011: "Every colour token is defined for light AND dark. No token may
        exist in only one theme — a missing dark value is a bug, not a
        fallback." Every key below therefore appears in all three dictionaries,
        and WinUI's ThemeDictionaries mechanism selects the set rather than
        falling through to a default. A guard test holds the three to the same
        keys (DesignSystemGuardTests).

        WHY THE BRUSHES ARE HERE TOO (Slice 1). A brush declared outside
        ThemeDictionaries is ONE object shared by every element, so it cannot
        follow a theme set on part of the tree at run time: the workspace's own
        Light/Dark choice sets the theme on the window's content, not on the
        application, which may only be set at start-up. Declared inside each
        theme dictionary, a brush is one object per theme and the element's
        theme picks it. This is the pattern WinUI's own dictionaries use. Each
        brush names its colour from the SAME dictionary, so the lookup never
        leaves it (ADR-011 rule 6).

        High contrast is first-class, not an afterthought: it maps to the
        SystemColor* resources the OS supplies, so the user's own high-contrast
        choice wins over anything Noto would pick.

        These are NOTO DESIGN VALUES. They are not SideNotes measurements —
        SideNotes is macOS-only and could not be observed (see the blocked
        quality-benchmark research). Nothing here claims parity with it.

        Roles, beyond the surfaces and text:
          Interaction*        Hover, Pressed, Selected, the text on a selected row, and
                              InteractionText: the text on a hovered or pressed surface.
                              In Light and Dark it is the primary text; in high contrast it
                              is HighlightText, because the hover fill there is Highlight and
                              the OS pairs the two (WinUI's own list does the same).
          SelectionIndicator  The pill on a selected row: the user's accent, as Windows'
                              own lists draw it (SystemAccentColorDark1 on light,
                              SystemAccentColorLight2 on dark). In high contrast,
                              HighlightText on the Highlight row.
          BorderFocus,        The two-tone keyboard focus ring Windows 11 draws: a dark
          BorderFocusInner    outer stroke and a light inner one in Light, the reverse in
                              Dark, so the ring is visible on every fill, the accent
                              included. In high contrast, WindowText and Window.
          SurfaceInput,       First instantiations of ADR-011's declared Input surface and
          TextDanger          Danger roles (M2-1's text input and validation message).
          InteractionPressed, Two more declared roles a button needs: Pressed is not
          TextDisabled        Selected, and Disabled is not Secondary.

        WinUI's own controls read their state colours from their own keys:
        TextControl* (TextBox), ComboBox*, ToggleSwitch*, and
        SystemControlFocusVisual* for the focus ring every native control draws.
        They are mapped to Noto's roles at the end of each dictionary
        (lightweight styling): every native control then has Noto's states
        without a copied template, and the focus ring has one source. Left as
        Windows draws them: a focused input's accent underline, a switch that
        is on (the accent), a list item's selection pill (the accent). In high
        contrast, a control with no "text on hover" key of its own shows hover
        as a Highlight border on the window colour, never as a Highlight fill
        under window text.
    -->

    <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

